Greedy Algorithm:
A greedy algorithm is an algorithmic technique that build the 
solution step by step always choosing the best possible option 
at the current moment(locally optimal choice), hopin that these choices
lead to the best overall globally optimal solution.


Real life example:
You are traveling from one city to another.


A -> J1->J2->J3->J4 -> B 

local optimal choices leads you to the Global optimal solution. 

The decision is always based on the current best option.


A greedy algorithm is an algorithm that
 i)Makes the best local choice
 ii)Never changes previous choices
 iii)Tries to obtail the global optimum
 
 
When it works:
Greedy algo works only if the problem stisfies two important properties 
i)Greedy choice property
ii)Optimal Substructure

Advantages:
Simle(Easy to understant)
Fast
Less Memory
Easy Implementation

Disadvanges:
Doesn't always work(Fails for many optimization problems)
Local Optimum 	 not produce best solution always.

Acivity Selection Problem:

Problem Statement:
N activities
Each activity has start time and finish time
A person can perform only one activity at a time.
Two activities are comatiple if they do not overlap.


Goal: Select the maximum number of non-overlapping activities.


Step 1:Sort activities according to finish time.
Step 2: select the first activity.
step 3: Move through the remaining activities
  choose activity only if 
  start time>=finish time of previous activity
  
  
  Acitivity  start  finish
  A1          1       4
  A2          3       5
  A3          0       6
  A4          5       7
  A5          8       9
  A6          5       9 
  
  Activities are already sorted.
  
  Intially 
  
  A1 , last finish time=4
  
  check A2,  3>=4 false  reject
  check A3,  0>=4 false  reject
  check A4,  5>=4 true   selected   last finish time=7
  check A5,  8>=7 true   selected   last finish time=9
  check A6,  5>=9 false  reject     
  
  Final answer: A1,A4,A5
  Maximum activity=3
  
  
  
  Job Scheduling Problem:
  Problem Statement:
  Suppose there are N jobs.
  
  Each job has id,deadline,profit
  
  conditions:
  i)Every job takes one unit of time
  ii)Only one job can be executed at a time.
  iii)A job must be completed before the deadline or on its deadline.
 
 
 Goal: Maximize total profit
 
 Which jobs should be selected to maximize the profit?
 
 
 Algorithm:
 Step 1: sort all jobs in decreasing order of their profit.
 step 2: Find maximum deadline.
 step 3: Process every job	
 
 
 Job    deadline   profit
 A        2         100
 B        1         19
 C        2         27
 D        1         25
 E        3         15 
 
 
 
 Job    deadline   profit
 A        2         100
 C        2         27
 D        1         25
 B        1         19
 E        3         15 
 
 find max deadline maxd=3
 
 
create slots - slot1, slot2, slot3  (initially all slots empty)


job A: deadline=2, profit=100
try latest possible slot

slot 2 empty-> Assign job A   [slot1-E, slot2-A, slot3-E ]



job C:deadline=2, profit=27

try slot2 -> occupied

so move backward
 slot1->empty
 
 Assign slot1=C  [slot1-A, slot2-A, slot3-E]
 
 
 
 job D:deadline=1, profit=25
 
 try slot 1 -> occupied
 
 Cannot schedule.
 
 
 
 Job B: deadline=1, profit=19
 try slot 1 -> occupied
 Cannot schedule.
 
 
 Job E: deadline=3, profit=15
 
try slot 3

Assign to slot3 [slot1-A, slot2-A, slot3-A]

[C,A,E] = 27+100+15=142(Maximum profit)



Fractional Knapsack problem:

You are given a knapsack with capacity w.
Given n items.
Each item has weight and value.

You can take complete/partial item.
Your goal is to maximize the total value inside the knapsack.


Example:

Items    weight    value
A         10        60
B         20       100
C         30       120


Knapsack capacity=50


step 1: calculate value per unit weight

value per unit weight=value/weight

Items    weight    value   ratio
A         10        60      6
B         20       100      5
C         30       120      4


step 2: Sort by highest ratio

descending order: A->B->C

step 3: Fill the knapsack

capacity=50

take item A  weight=10,  remaining capacity=50-10=40, profit=60
take item B  weight=20,  remaining capacity=40-20=20, profit=60+100=160
take item C  weight=30,  remaining capacity=20  can not take the full item

take fraction=20/30=2/3   profit=120*2/3=80  total profit=160+80=240


max profit=240



Huffman Encoding:

AAAAABBC - 10100


A - 01000001
B - 01000010
C - 01000011

A - 1
B - 01
C - 00

At every step, Huffman makes the locally optimal choice:

It assigns:
 Shorter binary codes to frequently occurring characters
 Longer binary codes to less frequently occurring characters
 
 Reduces the total number of bits  required too store data.
 
 characters
 A:5
 B:9
 C:12
 D:13
 E:16	
 F:45
 
 step 1:
 
 take two smallest
 A(5), B(9)
 
 
 c0mbine
 
  14
 / \
 A  B
 
 
 Step 2: 

12+13=25

   25
   /\
   C D
  
 step 3:
 
 combine: 14+16=30
 
   30
   /\
 14  E 



step 4: combine 25+30=55

step 5: combine 45+55=100


         100
         /\
        F 55
           /\
          25  30
          /\  / \
          C D 14 E
               /\
              A  B

LEFT=0 Right=1


Traverse from root

F=0
